7:T476,TSIG-PAPER-2025-CCKM (ePrint 2025/943, abstract) proves the two impossibility
results for key-unique schemes. TSIG-PAPER-2025-CKKTZ, Section 4.2 (CRYPTO
2025; ePrint 2025/1061, PDF pp. 16-18), gives an explicit adaptive attack,
which needs no signing queries and works even with erasures, on any threshold
Schnorr scheme whose secret shares are Shamir shares with computable
commitments, FROST included, whenever the LDVR problem is easy for the same
parameters; LDVR is unconditionally hard up to half the threshold and in some
regimes beyond (Theorem 3, Section 4.4, PDF pp. 27-28), and its hardness
elsewhere is unclear (Section 1.2, PDF p. 6). TSIG-PAPER-2025-MSFROST,
Theorem 2 (Section 5; EUROCRYPT 2026; ePrint 2025/1953, revision of
2026-09-02, PDF p. 24), rules out algebraic black-box reductions from AOMDL in
the random oracle model to adaptive TS-UF-0 security of ms-FROST, unless AOMDL
is easy; it covers the masked scheme ms-FROST, which has no public key shares
and so lies outside the key-unique scope of this barrier. These results
restrict proof techniques and scheme classes; the LDVR attack is conditional
on solving LDVR.8:T40a,Rows are the published mechanisms for threshold ML-DSA, plus Threshold
Raccoon as the strongest scheme with its own verifier and threshold FALCON,
which keeps the standard verifier of the other NIST-selected lattice
signature by running its signing algorithm in generic MPC. The threshold-FHE row
reaches every theory requirement in principle, which is why the goal also
asks for few rounds without homomorphic or generic secure evaluation of
signing. The THED verifier cell is partial because the construction is
described for round-3 Dilithium and FIPS 204 conformance was not checked. The
honest-majority row is marked no on the dishonest-majority column because it
tolerates \( t \) corruptions only among 2t + 1 parties. The FALCON row is
partial on thresholds because its back end is generic but only N-of-N and
three-party signing were implemented. There is no concrete column: no source
describes a deployment of threshold ML-DSA with a workload and a budget to
measure costs against (TSIG-PAPER-2026-NIST8214C, Appendix C.3).9:T487,A protocol and proof such that, for at least one FIPS 204 parameter set and for all \( 1 \le t \le n \le \mathrm{poly}(\lambda) \): (a) key generation (a trusted dealer is allowed) outputs an ML-DSA public key in FIPS 204 format and shares; (b) any set of at least \( t \) parties produces, in at most three rounds per attempt and with \( O(1) \) expected attempts (parallel attempts allowed), a signature accepted by unmodified ML-DSA.Verify; (c) per-party communication and computation are \( \mathrm{poly}(\lambda, n) \), including all preprocessing; (\( d \)) no party evaluates the signing algorithm, its hash functions, or its rejection test under fully homomorphic encryption or general-purpose secure computation; (e) threshold unforgeability holds in the random oracle model against a static malicious adversary corrupting up to \( t-1 \) parties and controlling the network, with abort allowed, under MLWE and SelfTargetMSIS or ML-DSA unforgeability plus lattice assumptions, with no heuristic about rejected transcripts, no trusted hardware, no correlated-randomness provider, and no key rotation below the signing-query bound of the parameter set.a:T5f2,ML-DSA signing rejects and retries until the response is short, and rejected
candidates must stay hidden (TSIG-BARRIER-002); ordinary Shamir shares of the
short secret are not short (TSIG-BARRIER-001). Combining many partial
responses also enlarges the norm of the final response, which ML-DSA's fixed
parameters do not tolerate (TSIG-PAPER-2026-QUORUS, §1). Each known protocol
gives up one requirement. TSIG-PAPER-2026-CELI uses short replicated secret
sharing and reaches [three rounds against a dishonest
majority](TSIG-OP-002), but its costs grow roughly as
\( \exp(O(T\sqrt{\binom{N}{T-1}})) \) and its parameters stop at six parties;
NIST notes that costs proportional to the number of quorums stop being
practical (TSIG-PAPER-2026-NIST8214C, Appendix C.3). TSIG-PAPER-2026-QUORUS
shows that, after a small change to signing, the high bits and challenge of a
rejected attempt can be revealed, so the hash is computed in the clear; it
then evaluates the rejection test with honest-majority MPC and handles any
number of parties, but [only with an honest majority and tens of
rounds](TSIG-OP-004). Threshold FHE gives [one-round signing in
principle](TSIG-OP-005) (TSIG-PAPER-2018-BGGJKRS) and two rounds concretely
in TSIG-PAPER-2026-THED, which is proved against passive adversaries, assumes
that aborted transcripts are harmless, and needs about a second of GPU time
per attempt. For lattice signatures designed for thresholds, [three rounds
for large thresholds](TSIG-OP-006) are known (TSIG-PAPER-2024-RACCOON).b:T5a5,Fix a FIPS 204 parameter set with algorithms
\( (\mathsf{KeyGen}, \mathsf{Sign}, \mathsf{Verify}) \). A threshold scheme
consists of \( \mathsf{TKeyGen}(1^\lambda, t, n) \to (pk, sk_1, \dots, sk_n) \),
where \( pk \) is an ML-DSA public key (a trusted dealer is allowed), and an
interactive protocol \( \Pi_{\mathsf{Sign}} \) run by any \( S \subseteq [n] \)
with \( |S| \ge t \) on message \( m \).

- Interchangeability: whenever \( \Pi_{\mathsf{Sign}} \) outputs \( \sigma \),
  \( \mathsf{Verify}(pk, m, \sigma) = 1 \).
- Rounds: each attempt uses at most three rounds; the expected number of
  attempts is \( O(1) \), and attempts may run in parallel.
- Cost: per-party communication and computation are
  \( \mathrm{poly}(\lambda, n) \) for all \( 1 \le t \le n \le
  \mathrm{poly}(\lambda) \), counting all preprocessing, and no party
  evaluates the signing algorithm, its hash functions, or its rejection test
  under fully homomorphic encryption or general-purpose secure computation.
- Security: a PPT adversary statically corrupts \( C \subseteq [n] \) with
  \( |C| \le t-1 \), controls the network, may make honest parties abort,
  and wins if it outputs a valid signature on a message that no honest party
  agreed to sign. Its advantage is negligible in the random oracle model under
  MLWE and SelfTargetMSIS (or ML-DSA unforgeability and lattice assumptions),
  for the number of signing queries supported by the parameter set.c:T497,Early distributed lattice signatures were full-threshold or two-party
(TSIG-PAPER-2021-DOTT, TSIG-PAPER-2022-DILIZIUM2) or targeted
threshold-friendly schemes with their own verifiers (TSIG-PAPER-2023-GKS,
TSIG-PAPER-2024-TANG, TSIG-PAPER-2024-RACCOON, TSIG-PAPER-2024-AOMMLWE).
TSIG-PAPER-2018-BGGJKRS had already shown that threshold FHE turns any
deterministic signature scheme into a one-round threshold scheme by evaluating
signing under threshold FHE. TSIG-PAPER-2026-NIST8214C made ML-DSA signing a target of
NIST's threshold call, with interchangeability required. In 2026 three
ML-DSA-compatible protocols appeared: short replicated sharing for up to six
parties (TSIG-PAPER-2026-CELI), honest-majority MPC (TSIG-PAPER-2026-QUORUS),
and a concrete threshold-FHE protocol (TSIG-PAPER-2026-THED), after which
TSIG-PAPER-2026-PASSELEGUE stated the remaining combination as open. For
FALCON, the other NIST-selected lattice signature, the first protocol with
the standard verifier evaluates signing in generic MPC: for four parties
against three malicious ones, a Falcon-512 signature takes 7.66 s online,
amortized over 2000 signatures (TSIG-PAPER-2026-GEPTW, Table 3).d:T47e,TSIG-PAPER-2018-BGGJKRS builds threshold FHE from LWE and a universal
thresholdizer: after a trusted setup, the parties hold an FHE encryption of
the signing key, evaluate a deterministic signing algorithm on it
homomorphically, and send partial decryptions, any \( t \) of which reveal an
ordinary signature of the base scheme (§8.2). Each partial decryption carries
a zero-knowledge proof, with preprocessing, that it matches a committed key
share, so malformed contributions are detected (§7.1). Unforgeability is
proved against an adversary that receives the keys of \( t-1 \) parties,
chosen before its signing queries (Theorem 8.20). TSIG-PAPER-2026-PASSELEGUE
(§4.1) notes that this one-round construction applies to Dilithium, and FIPS
204 includes a deterministic mode of ML-DSA (TSIG-PAPER-2026-NIST8214C,
Appendix A.1.4); the thesis also notes that proofs of correct partial
decryption give malicious security (§4.4). The concrete instantiation
TSIG-PAPER-2026-THED modifies the construction to sign in two rounds for
every \( t \) and \( n \), proves it against passive adversaries, and
reports about a second of GPU time per attempt.e:T46e,For FROST itself, TSIG-PAPER-2025-CKKTZ proves adaptive security in the random
oracle model only for [about half the threshold](TSIG-OP-008); full
adaptivity needs the algebraic group model and a new assumption, LDVR, whose
hardness it shows is necessary for a broad class of threshold Schnorr schemes.
For key-unique schemes, in which each public key share fixes its secret share,
TSIG-PAPER-2025-CCKM shows that adaptive security beyond about half the
threshold cannot be proved from one-more discrete logarithm assumptions with
natural rewinding reductions in the random oracle model (TSIG-BARRIER-003).
The schemes that get around these results each give up one requirement:
[the algebraic group model](TSIG-OP-009) (TSIG-PAPER-2025-MSFROST,
TSIG-PAPER-2025-FAFROST), [periodic refresh of key shares](TSIG-OP-010)
(TSIG-PAPER-2025-GLRS), [a third round or state across
sessions](TSIG-OP-011), or [a signature with an extra
scalar](TSIG-OP-012) (both TSIG-PAPER-2025-CHEN). TSIG-PAPER-2025-MSFROST
also shows that no black-box algebraic reduction from AOMDL in the random
oracle model proves its two-round scheme adaptively secure.f:T596,Builds threshold fully homomorphic encryption from LWE in two ways: with linear secret sharing whose reconstruction coefficients are binary, which covers every threshold access structure, and with Shamir sharing and noise that tolerates multiplication by Lagrange coefficients (Sections 5.2-5.3). From it, with zero-knowledge proofs with preprocessing and commitments, builds a universal thresholdizer whose partial evaluations can be publicly verified (Section 7.1). Applied to a signature scheme with deterministic signing, which the authors take to be without loss of generality, it gives a one-round threshold signature: a trusted setup encrypts the signing key under threshold FHE and hands out key shares, each party homomorphically evaluates signing of message m on the encrypted key and returns one partial decryption with a proof, and any t of them combine into an ordinary signature of the base scheme (Construction 8.16). Unforgeability holds against an adversary that receives the keys of a maximal invalid party set, chosen after it sees the public parameters and before its adaptive signing queries, if the thresholdizer is secure and the base scheme is unforgeable (Definition 8.13, Theorem 8.20); robustness and anonymity follow from the thresholdizer (Theorems 8.21-8.22). With LWE-based signatures, the authors obtain the first one-round threshold signature from LWE that is succinct and anonymous (Section 1.1).10:T437,ePrint 2017/956 PDF (the PDF states no revision date; PDF metadata dated 2017-09-27; downloaded 2026-10-11; printed and PDF page numbers coincide): title-page note (p. 1) for the merge of ePrint 2017/251 and 2017/257, which the PDF cites as separate reports (pp. 39, 41); Section 1.1 (pp. 2-3) for the one-round threshold signature from LWE; Theorem 3.10 (p. 11) and Section 5.2.1 (p. 19) for the LWE parameters, with modulus \( 2^{\tilde O(d)} \) for circuit depth d; Sections 5.2-5.4 (pp. 17-20) for the threshold FHE constructions; Section 3.3.1 (pp. 8-9) and Construction 7.7 with Theorems 7.8-7.12 (Section 7.1, pp. 26-27) for the universal thresholdizer with proofs of correct partial decryption; Definitions 8.9-8.15, Construction 8.16 and Theorems 8.17-8.22 (Section 8.2, pp. 31-33) for the threshold signature. TSIG-PAPER-2026-PASSELEGUE, Section 4.1 (author PDF p. 52), describes the generic one-round construction for deterministic signature schemes, notes that its signatures are interchangeable with the base scheme, and states that it could be applied to Dilithium.11:T414,ePrint 2019/114 (local PDF, revised version): abstract (PDF p. 1); Section 1, Our Result (PDF p. 2), contrasting the costly distributed key generation of the threshold-optimal schemes [4, 17] and the limits of 2-out-of-2 schemes [12, 28]; Section 1.1 (PDF pp. 2–3) for the multiplicative-sharing approach of [12, 28, 30] being hard to generalize beyond t = 2 and the SPDZ-inspired pairwise share conversion; Section 1.2 (PDF p. 3), "Following [28]", for the optimistic signature check; Section 2 (PDF p. 4) for static corruption of up to n − 1 players and no robustness; Section 4.1 (PDF p. 10) for key generation by per-player Feldman VSS; Section 4.2 (PDF pp. 11–12) for signing; Theorem 1 (PDF p. 12) for unforgeability under DSA unforgeability, Strong RSA, non-malleable equivocable commitments, and DDH; Section 4.9 (PDF pp. 19–20) for the simulation strategy that follows Lindell; Sections 6.2–6.3 (PDF p. 24) for instantiating the conversion with DKLS18's OT-based multiplier. Reference [12] is DKLS18 and [28] is Lindell 2017.12:T601,Asks for a round-optimal Schnorr threshold signature with full adaptive security under well-established assumptions, for arbitrarily large signing groups, with identifiable aborts (Section 1). Gives FaFROST, a FROST2 variant that keeps the message-independent first round and masks each second-round partial signature with pairwise blinding values derived from pairwise keys, the first-round messages, the message and the signer set, and FaFROST+, which adds Pedersen-committed key shares and an identification protocol run only after a failed signing session, assuming a PKI and authenticated channels. Signatures verify as standard Schnorr signatures. With trusted key generation, Theorem 1 proves FaFROST \( (t_c, t, n) \)-adaptively unforgeable for all \( t_c < t \le n \) against algebraic adversaries that corrupt signers at any time and learn their keys and stored state, under AOMDL with the hash functions modeled as random oracles; the bound includes a term \( q_{sig} \cdot t/(p(t-t_c)) \). The unforgeability notion is TS-UF-1, and Section 4 argues, after Katsumata, Reichle and Takemure, that TS-UF-4 follows for schemes of this kind. Theorem 2 extends adaptive unforgeability to FaFROST+ given an equivocable trapdoor commitment and a zero-knowledge NIZK, and Theorem 3 proves identifiable aborts. Section 2.1 explains why a rewinding proof fails for this challenge embedding, and Section 3 states that ms-FROST of TSIG-PAPER-2025-MSFROST is the same signature scheme, proposed concurrently and without identifiable aborts.13:T581,The PDF is the full version of the ASIACRYPT 2026 paper; its title-page note (p. 1) states that it is a major revision of, and supersedes, the preprint first posted under the title "Fully Adaptive FROST in the Algebraic Group Model From Falsifiable Assumptions". Section 3 (p. 11) thanks the authors of TSIG-PAPER-2025-MSFROST for pointing out an error in an earlier version of the proof, which did not account for an additional adversarial winning probability \( q_{sig} \cdot t/(p(t-t_c)) \) that corresponds to the wtROS problem in their proof; the current proof handles it with a combinatorial argument inspired by wtROS, without wtROS as a separate assumption (Section 6, pp. 28-32). The acknowledgments (p. 37) add that K. Takemure and N. Kohen pointed out that the earlier analysis omitted a step needed to account for ROS attacks in the simulation and missed the adversary's several ways of turning the challenge matrix into a forgery matrix; the fix raises the reduction's failure probability by a factor \( t/(t-t_c) \le t \), and the ms-FROST authors found the second issue independently. TSIG-PAPER-2025-MSFROST (ePrint 2025/1953, revision of 2026-09-02, p. 3) locates the error on pp. 19-20 of the initial version, a claim that a value β is independent of the random oracle output c*, and states that a later version corrected it. Page numbers are PDF pages; the printed page number is one less.14:T57a,ePrint 2025/1950 PDF (the 2026-09-14 revision by its PDF metadata; downloaded 2026-10-11): title-page note (p. 1) for the version history; Section 1 (p. 3) for the question and contributions; Section 2.1 (pp. 6-8) for the proof strategy and why rewinding fails; Section 2.2 (pp. 8-11) for identifiable aborts with a PKI and authenticated channels; Section 3 (p. 11) for the concurrent ms-FROST and the corrected error, and Table 1 (p. 12) for fully adaptive threshold Schnorr schemes by rounds, model, assumption and identifiable aborts; Figure 2 (p. 14) and Definitions 1-2 (pp. 15-16) for the security games, in which corruption reveals the key and state and at most \( t_c \) corruptions are allowed, and for TS-UF-1 (p. 15); Figures 4-6 (pp. 18-20) for the scheme; Theorem 1 (Section 6, p. 18), with trusted key generation (p. 19), for FaFROST; Lemmas 1-2 (pp. 30-31) for the corrected bound; Theorems 2 and 3 (pp. 32-33) for FaFROST+; acknowledgments (p. 37) for the issues found in the earlier proof. NIST TCPT2 preview slides (https://csrc.nist.gov/presentations/2026/tcpt2-1b2): slide 2 for the goal (standard Schnorr verification, full adaptivity, two rounds, established assumptions, identifiable aborts), slide 22 for the preprocessable first round, and slide 25 for the comparison table of fully adaptive threshold Schnorr schemes by rounds, model and assumption, with the note on ms-FROST.15:T518,Calls full adaptive security against up to \( t-1 \) corruptions under standard assumptions a longstanding and unresolved challenge for threshold Schnorr, since recent impossibility results rule out known proof techniques for widely deployed two-round schemes or rely on speculative assumptions and idealized models, while fully adaptive constructions from falsifiable assumptions need more rounds; it poses the question of a fully adaptively secure scheme from falsifiable assumptions with minimal round complexity. Gives a two-round, stateless threshold Schnorr scheme with deterministic signing whose signatures verify as standard Schnorr signatures, and proves it unforgeable against an adversary that adaptively corrupts up to \( t-1 \) parties, under DDH in the random oracle model (Theorem 1): for at most d+1 first-round signing queries per signer (Theorem 2), and for polynomially many signatures in a relaxed model where each signer refreshes the commitment part of its partial public key with a succinct stateless token after every d signing queries (Theorem 3). The commitment scheme and proof system are instantiated with Pedersen commitments and inner-product arguments that add no assumption beyond DDH. The proof uses a technique the authors call equivocal deterministic nonce derivation.16:T456,USENIX Security 2026 final proceedings PDF: abstract and Section 1.1 (pp. 6305-6306) for ML-DSA compatibility, a posteriori sharing, DKG, and "parameters for signing up to 6 parties"; Table 1 (p. 6307) for the comparison with Bienstock et al., Trilithium and generic MPC; Section 2.2 (p. 6308) for static security and the 5-bit complexity-leveraging loss for \( N \le 6 \); Section 3.1 (p. 6311) for the trusted dealer of Fig. 5 with the DKG deferred to the full version; Remarks 2 and 3 (p. 6312) for the offline first round and the hyperball signature distribution with the same support as ML-DSA; Theorem 3.2 (p. 6314) for unforgeability; Section 3.5 and Table 3 (p. 6317) for the \( N \le 6 \) choice and the 6 MB (\( N = 7 \)) and 70 MB (\( N = 8 \)) worst-case costs; Table 7 (p. 6319) for the comparison with Threshold Raccoon and Ringtail. Mithril preview writeup (https://csrc.nist.gov/csrc/media/Projects/threshold-cryptography/documents/TCall-1/Mithril-PW01.pdf), Sections 2.2-2.3 (printed p. 2): practical up to about N = 8, limited by exponential share growth; no identifiable abort or robustness.17:T542,- **Threshold convention**: papers use \(t\) either for the number of tolerated
  corruptions, so that \(t+1\) parties sign, or for the number of required
  signers. The \(n\)-out-of-\(n\) case is called full threshold.
- **Key generation**: a trusted dealer shares the key, or a distributed key
  generation (DKG) protocol lets the parties create their shares and the
  public key so that no party ever holds the key (TSIG-PAPER-1991-PEDERSEN,
  TSIG-PAPER-1999-GJKR-DKG).
- **Robustness and identifiable abort**: a robust protocol outputs a valid
  signature despite malicious participants; with identifiable abort, a failed
  run names a cheater; otherwise the protocol may simply abort.
- **Static, adaptive, proactive**: the adversary fixes its corrupted set before
  key generation or chooses it during execution; proactive refresh renews
  shares across epochs against an adversary that moves between parties.
- **Rounds and preprocessing**: online rounds after the message is known are
  counted separately from message-independent preprocessing; some schemes let
  each party send its signature share without interaction.
- **Interoperability**: the combined signature is accepted by the unmodified
  verifier of the base scheme (for example ECDSA, Schnorr/EdDSA, BLS, or
  ML-DSA), so a verifier need not know that a threshold was used.18:T6cc,- **Non-interactive shares for algebraic schemes**: threshold RSA
  (TSIG-PAPER-1996-GJKR-RSA, TSIG-PAPER-2000-SHOUP) and threshold BLS
  (TSIG-PAPER-2003-BOLDYREVA), where a combiner checks and interpolates
  signature shares.
- **Threshold Schnorr**: the shared nonce is the main difficulty. FROST signs
  in two rounds, or one online round after preprocessing
  (TSIG-PAPER-2020-FROST); later work proves FROST-type signing without the
  algebraic group model (TSIG-PAPER-2023-OLAF), gives stateless deterministic
  signing (TSIG-PAPER-2021-GKMN), and obtains full adaptive security from DDH
  in the random-oracle model (TSIG-PAPER-2024-GLACIUS).
- **Threshold ECDSA**: the inversion in ECDSA signing requires secure
  multiplication, built from Paillier encryption (TSIG-PAPER-2017-LINDELL,
  TSIG-PAPER-2018-GG, TSIG-PAPER-2020-CGGMP) or oblivious transfer
  (TSIG-PAPER-2019-DKLS-N). TSIG-PAPER-2020-CGGMP adds UC security against
  adaptive corruption, identifiable abort, and proactive refresh.
- **Lattice threshold signatures**: abort-based lattice signing leaks through
  rejected attempts and needs short shares. Approaches include two-round
  full-threshold signing (TSIG-PAPER-2021-DOTT), threshold homomorphic
  encryption for arbitrary thresholds (TSIG-PAPER-2023-GKS), Raccoon with
  one-time additive masks (TSIG-PAPER-2024-RACCOON), hash-and-sign via noise
  flooding (TSIG-PAPER-2024-FLOOD), two rounds without FHE under algebraic
  one-more LWE (TSIG-PAPER-2024-AOMMLWE), and an implemented scheme with
  ordinary-verifier output and proactive refresh (TSIG-PAPER-2024-TANG).
  Threshold ML-DSA protocols target the standardized verifier directly
  (TSIG-PAPER-2026-CELI, TSIG-PAPER-2026-QUORUS, TSIG-PAPER-2026-THED).19:T5dd,Parameters below occur in efficiency or security bounds. Each meaning is
scoped to its stated regime. Measured quantities are named in words; protocol
objects are defined where used. Source aliases do not equate different regimes.

| Symbol | Meaning | Regime | Paper aliases |
|---|---|---|---|
| \(n\) | Total number of key-share holders. | Threshold signing. | TSIG-PAPER-2020-FROST writes \(n\); TSIG-PAPER-2024-RACCOON writes \(N\); TSIG-PAPER-2000-SHOUP writes \(l\) |
| \(t\) | Minimum number of shares/signers required to sign. | Threshold signing; this is not the tolerated-corruption count. | TSIG-PAPER-2020-FROST writes \(t\); TSIG-PAPER-2024-RACCOON writes \(T\); TSIG-PAPER-2025-CKKTZ writes \(t+1\); TSIG-PAPER-2000-SHOUP writes \(k\) |
| \(t_c\) | Number of tolerated corruptions, as stated by the security theorem. | Threshold signing; it need not equal \(t-1\). | TSIG-PAPER-2025-CKKTZ writes \(t_c\); TSIG-PAPER-2025-FAFROST writes \(t_c\) |
| \(\lambda\) | Computational security parameter. | Threshold-signature security. | No paper spelling asserted. |
| \(q\) | Prime order of the signing group. | Schnorr groups. | TSIG-PAPER-2020-FROST writes \(q\) |
| \(d\) | Depth of the circuit evaluated inside the generic threshold construction. | BGGJKRS circuit-based construction. | No paper spelling asserted. |
